{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"b4970084bdcbfd519906a69ce5d0fd8ba5c8fd1846d4b612994c64ba4f9e4bfc","slug":"midlatitude-cyclone-pre-environment","location":"/weather_technology/midlat_cyclone/midlatitude_cyclone_pre_environment.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Pre-Environment Stage","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Kevin Goebbert","given":"Kevin","family":"Goebbert"},"name":"Kevin Goebbert","affiliations":["University of Tampa"],"id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","note":"All non-code content is licensed under CC-BY-4.0","free":true,"CC":true},"code":{"id":"Apache-2.0","url":"https://opensource.org/licenses/Apache-2.0","name":"Apache License 2.0","note":"All code content is licensed under Apache-2.0","free":true,"osi":true}},"github":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook","edit_url":null,"numbering":{"title":{"enabled":false,"offset":2},"equation":{"enabled":false},"figure":{"enabled":true,"continue":true}},"keywords":["metpy","python","meteorology","weather"],"copyright":"2026","affiliations":[{"id":"University of Tampa","name":"University of Tampa"}],"settings":{"output_stderr":"remove-warn","output_matplotlib_strings":"remove"},"source_url":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook/blob/main/weather_technology/midlat_cyclone/midlatitude_cyclone_pre_environment.md","thumbnail":"/metpy_thebook/_public/midlat_cyclone_pre_e-403623ff256bfcdc05576327e2af653b.png","thumbnailOptimized":"/metpy_thebook/_public/midlat_cyclone_pre_e-403623ff256bfcdc05576327e2af653b.webp","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"midlatitude_cyclone_pre_environment.md","url":"/metpy_thebook/_public/midlatitude_cyclone_-76cd8dc5e12ca0f51d01f0b48b95bb4f.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most cyclones form over an area that has at least some kind of surface\ntemperature gradient. As you will learn about later in your meteorology\ncoursework, a surface temperature gradient is not absolutely needed to\nform a cyclone. However, cyclones will preferentially form over regions\nthat have some weak temperature gradient, as it is easier for the\natmosphere to concentrate a weak temperature gradient rather than\nforming a temperature gradient where there was none.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"TuJzeJZXte"}],"key":"MTU2yreOvj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The pre-environment stage is defined by a weak temperature gradient (if\none exists at all), where there may or may not be defined a stationary\nboundary (","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"BkVcQB3Phg"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"preenv","label":"preenv","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fig. ","key":"r8A8osUvA8"},{"type":"text","value":"25","key":"zBtu5990tv"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"25","resolved":true,"html_id":"preenv","key":"AheL0kIVDV"},{"type":"text","value":"). In general, there is little temperature advection\nnear the surface and only a small depression of lower pressure. In\ngeneral, there is also a moisture gradient between the two different\nairmasses. In the Northern Hemisphere to the north is the dry\ncontinental airmass and to the south the moist tropical airmass.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"ihIPaKjZgL"}],"key":"KLz8RoQbnd"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Associated with the temperature gradient at the surface there is a need\nfor an upper-level trough to initiate the cyclogenesis stage\n(","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"g7TDslQp1T"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"preenv","label":"preenv","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fig. ","key":"iQ7f1vubpB"},{"type":"text","value":"25","key":"vZmGqxKq7K"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"25","resolved":true,"html_id":"preenv","key":"EPGzFm0jwr"},{"type":"text","value":").\nThis additional requirement differs from the classic Norwegian Cyclone\nModel (NCM) because of now having upper-level observations. The\nupper-level trough will initiate appropriate divergence and subsequent\nrising motion to the ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"UV2TJwHRj4"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"east","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"tkRYb2A80v"}],"key":"d8dTpfEVlu"},{"type":"text","value":" of the trough axis. This will eventually\nresult in less mass within the column, which will mean the surface\npressure will decrease, initiating our mid-latitude cyclone.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"NSxG0rqyPz"}],"key":"rooRr3wHqY"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/metpy_thebook/_public/midlat_cyclone_pre_e-403623ff256bfcdc05576327e2af653b.png","alt":"Pre-environment of Mid-latitude Cyclone","width":"650px","align":"center","key":"KTtaZskvz6","urlSource":"../../images/midlat_cyclone_pre_environment.png","urlOptimized":"/metpy_thebook/_public/midlat_cyclone_pre_e-403623ff256bfcdc05576327e2af653b.webp"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"preenv","identifier":"preenv","html_id":"preenv","enumerator":"25","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"wQbdjcnZmm"},{"type":"text","value":"25","key":"i78iTbXfB3"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"Ztiqhv7g4k"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"P41i9xMHM9"},{"type":"text","value":"A schematic diagram of the pre-environmental conditions over\nthe region where cyclogenesis will take place at the surface and\n500 hPa with location of low-level fronts for spatial reference.","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"DHzs6CcLo5"}],"key":"ONYfDeiOFn"}],"key":"H2o5hcZ2ye"}],"label":"preenv","identifier":"preenv","enumerator":"25","html_id":"preenv","key":"WQGxEjRMf8"}],"key":"K4LDXpLSM6"}],"key":"UyUQLspsij"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Life Cycle of Mid-Latitude Cyclones","url":"/midlatitude-cyclone-intro","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"},"next":{"title":"Cyclogenesis Stage","url":"/midlatitude-cyclone-cyclogenesis","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"}}},"domain":"http://pre.render"}